  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King kept them in a game they couldn't finish.",
    "what_this_means": "A pitching duel stripped down to one run on each side through six innings, then broke open in the seventh when St. Louis pulled ahead. The Padres scratched out their single run early. Bogaerts drove one in. But nine strikeouts silenced the rest of the lineup. King delivered six innings of control, one earned run, six strikeouts. That was enough to win most nights. Not this one. They had traffic in two RISP situations and converted once. The other chance died. This is the math they're working with: excellent pitching, nine strikeouts, one successful at-bat with runners in scoring position. That adds up to a loss when the other team scores two.",
    "what_to_watch": "The fragility of this team is no longer theory. They sit one game back in the division, and every game now tests whether the formula. Sharp pitching, thin margins. Holds under pressure. Last night it didn't. Good pitching only matters if something else happens first. The bats have to answer. They didn't answer often enough, and King's night became a bridge to a game that got away. Nine strikeouts against one run on runners in scoring position is not a slump. It's a warning light.",
